.6,-130.39999999999998 244,-130.99999999999994 244.4,-131.60000000000002 244.8,-132.2 245.2,-132.8 245.6,-133.39999999999998 246,-134.00000000000006 246.4,-134.60000000000002 246.8,-135.19999999999993 247.2,-135.79999999999995 247.60000000000002,-136.39999999999998 248,-137 248.4,-137.59999999999997 248.79999999999998,-138.2 249.20000000000002,-138.8 249.60000000000002,-139.39999999999998 250,-140 250.39999999999998,-140.60000000000002 250.79999999999998,-141.2 251.20000000000002,-141.8 251.6,-142.40000000000003 252,-143 252.39999999999998,-143.60000000000002 252.8,-144.20000000000005 253.2,-144.80000000000007 253.6,-145.39999999999998 254,-145.99999999999994 254.4,-146.60000000000002 254.8,-147.2 255.2,-147.8 255.6,-148.39999999999998 256,-149.00000000000006 256.4,-149.60000000000002 256.8,-150.19999999999993 257.2,-150.79999999999995 257.6,-151.39999999999998 258,-152 258.4,-152.59999999999997 258.79999999999995,-153.2 259.20000000000005,-153.8 259.6,-154.39999999999998 260,-155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></polyline><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><foreignObject x=\"180\" y=\"100\" width=\"70\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"124\" y=\"86\" width=\"28\" height=\"18\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"180\" y=\"60\" width=\"28\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></div></foreignObject></svg><figcaption><math><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"The Newton quotient ``\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}`` is actually the quotient of two quantities, ``\\Delta y`` and ``\\Delta x``. It is not at all clear, however, that the derivative ``\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}``, the limit of ``\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}`` as ``\\Delta x`` approaches zero, can be regarded as a quotient. If ``y`` is a continuous function of ``x``, then ``\\Delta y`` approaches zero when ``\\Delta x`` approaches zero, so ``\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}`` appears to be the meaningless quantity ``\\frac{0}{0}``. Nevertheless, it is sometimes useful to be able to refer to quantities ``\\mathrm d y`` and ``\\mathrm d x`` in such a way that their quotient is the derivative ``\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}``. We can justify this by regarding ``\\mathrm d x`` as a new independent variable (called the *differential of ``x``*) and defining a new dependent variable ``\\mathrm d y`` (the *differential of ``y``*) as a function of ``x`` and ``\\mathrm d x`` by\n\n```math\n\\mathrm{d} y=\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\mathrm d x=f^\\prime(x)\\,\\mathrm d x\n```\n\nFor example, if ``y=x^2``, we can write ``\\mathrm{d} y=2x\\,\\mathrm{d} x`` to mean the same thing as ``\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}=2x``. Similarly, if ``f\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}``, we can write ``\\mathrm{d} y=-\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d} x`` as the equivalent differential form of the assertion that ``\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}=-\\frac{1}{x^2}``. This *differential notation* is useful in applications, and especially for the interpretation and manipulation of integrals. Note that, defined as above, differentials are merely variables that may or may not be small in absolute value.\n\n### Approximation by Differentials\n\nIf one quantity, say ``y``, is a function of another quantity ``x``, that is,\n```math\ny=f\\left(x\\right)\\,,\n```\nwe sometimes want to know how a change in the value of ``x`` by an amount ``\\Delta x`` will affect the value of ``y``. The exact change ``\\Delta y`` in ``y`` is given by\n```math\n\\Delta y=f\\left(x+\\Delta x\\right)-f\\left(x\\right)\\,,\n```\nbut if the change ``\\Delta x`` is small, then we can get a good approximation to ``\\Delta y`` by using the fact that ``\\displaystyle\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}`` is approximately the derivative ``\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}``. Thus,\n```math\n\\Delta y=\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}\\Delta x\\approx\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\Delta x=f^\\prime\\left(x\\right)\\Delta x\\,.\n```\nIt is often convenient to represent this approximation in terms of differentials; if we denote the change in ``x`` by ``\\mathrm{d} x`` instead of ``\\Delta x``, then the change ``\\Delta y`` in ``y`` is approximated by the differential ``\\mathrm{d} y``, that is\n```math\n\\Delta y\\approx\\mathrm{d} y=f^\\prime\\left(x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\\,.","output":"","result":"The Newton quotient ``\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}`` is actually the quotient of two quantities, ``\\Delta y`` and ``\\Delta x``. It is not at all clear, however, that the derivative ``\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}``, the limit of ``\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}`` as ``\\Delta x`` approaches zero, can be regarded as a quotient. If ``y`` is a continuous function of ``x``, then ``\\Delta y`` approaches zero when ``\\Delta x`` approaches zero, so ``\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}`` appears to be the meaningless quantity ``\\frac{0}{0}``. Nevertheless, it is sometimes useful to be able to refer to quantities ``\\mathrm d y`` and ``\\mathrm d x`` in such a way that their quotient is the derivative ``\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}``. We can justify this by regarding ``\\mathrm d x`` as a new independent variable (called the *differential of ``x``*) and defining a new dependent variable ``\\mathrm d y`` (the *differential of ``y``*) as a function of ``x`` and ``\\mathrm d x`` by\n\n```math\n\\mathrm{d} y=\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\mathrm d x=f^\\prime(x)\\,\\mathrm d x\n```\n\nFor example, if ``y=x^2``, we can write ``\\mathrm{d} y=2x\\,\\mathrm{d} x`` to mean the same thing as ``\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}=2x``. Similarly, if ``f\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}``, we can write ``\\mathrm{d} y=-\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d} x`` as the equivalent differential form of the assertion that ``\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}=-\\frac{1}{x^2}``. This *differential notation* is useful in applications, and especially for the interpretation and manipulation of integrals. Note that, defined as above, differentials are merely variables that may or may not be small in absolute value.\n\n### Approximation by Differentials\n\nIf one quantity, say ``y``, is a function of another quantity ``x``, that is,\n```math\ny=f\\left(x\\right)\\,,\n```\nwe sometimes want to know how a change in the value of ``x`` by an amount ``\\Delta x`` will affect the value of ``y``. The exact change ``\\Delta y`` in ``y`` is given by\n```math\n\\Delta y=f\\left(x+\\Delta x\\right)-f\\left(x\\right)\\,,\n```\nbut if the change ``\\Delta x`` is small, then we can get a good approximation to ``\\Delta y`` by using the fact that ``\\displaystyle\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}`` is approximately the derivative ``\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}``. Thus,\n```math\n\\Delta y=\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}\\Delta x\\approx\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\Delta x=f^\\prime\\left(x\\right)\\Delta x\\,.\n```\nIt is often convenient to represent this approximation in terms of differentials; if we denote the change in ``x`` by ``\\mathrm{d} x`` instead of ``\\Delta x``, then the change ``\\Delta y`` in ``y`` is approximated by the differential ``\\mathrm{d} y``, that is\n```math\n\\Delta y\\approx\\mathrm{d} y=f^\\prime\\left(x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\\,.","resultHTML":"<p>The Newton quotient <math><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> is actually the quotient of two quantities, <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. It is not at all clear, however, that the derivative <math><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>, the limit of <math><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> as <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> approaches zero, can be regarded as a quotient. If <math><mi>y</mi></math> is a continuous function of <math><mi>x</mi></math>, then <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> approaches zero when <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> approaches zero, so <math><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> appears to be the meaningless quantity <math><mfrac><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn></mfrac></math>. Nevertheless, it is sometimes useful to be able to refer to quantities <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> in such a way that their quotient is the derivative <math><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. We can justify this by regarding <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> as a new independent variable (called the <em>differential of <math><mi>x</mi></math></em>) and defining a new dependent variable <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> (the <em>differential of <math><mi>y</mi></math></em>) as a function of <math><mi>x</mi></math> and <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> by</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo class=\"tml-prime tml-lrg-pad\">′</mo></msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>For example, if <math><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, we can write <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> to mean the same thing as <math><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Similarly, if <math><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow></math>, we can write <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> as the equivalent differential form of the assertion that <math><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></math>. This <em>differential notation</em> is useful in applications, and especially for the interpretation and manipulation of integrals. Note that, defined as above, differentials are merely variables that may or may not be small in absolute value.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-y2ubvb-1529-8wun07-approximation-by-differentials\">Approximation by Differentials</h3>\n<p>If one quantity, say <math><mi>y</mi></math>, is a function of another quantity <math><mi>x</mi></math>, that is,</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo></mrow></math>\n<p>we sometimes want to know how a change in the value of <math><mi>x</mi></math> by an amount <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> will affect the value of <math><mi>y</mi></math>. The exact change <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in <math><mi>y</mi></math> is given by</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo></mrow></math>\n<p>but if the change <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is small, then we can get a good approximation to <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> by using the fact that <math><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></math> is approximately the derivative <math><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. Thus,</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo class=\"tml-prime tml-lrg-pad\">′</mo></msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>It is often convenient to represent this approximation in terms of differentials; if we denote the change in <math><mi>x</mi></math> by <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> instead of <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, then the change <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in <math><mi>y</mi></math> is approximated by the differential <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, that is</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo class=\"tml-prime tml-lrg-pad\">′</mo></msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const scale = 40;\nconst xmid = scale;\nconst ymid = 3 * scale;\n\nconst f = (x) => 0.1 * (x + 0.5) ** 2 + 0.5;\nconst x0 = 2;\nconst fx0 = f(x0);\nconst x = 3.5;\nconst fx = f(x);\nconst tangentSlope = 0.2 * (x0 + 0.5);\nconst tangentAtX = tangentSlope * (x - x0) + fx0;\n\nconst figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width: 6 * scale,\n    height: 3.5 * scale+10,\n    caption: \"\\\\(\\\\mathrm dy\\\\), the change in height to the tangent line, approximates \\\\(Δy\\\\) the change in height to the graph of \\\\(f\\\\).\",\n});\n\nfigure.axes({\n    origin: [xmid, ymid],\n    scale,\n    x: {\n        ticks: [\n            { value: x0, label: \"x\", length: 1 },\n            { value: 4.75, label: String.raw`x+\\mathrm{d} x`, length: 10 },\n        ],\n    },\n    y: { ticks: [] },\n});\n\nfigure.plot(f, {\n    domain: [-1, 5],\n    samples: 601,\n    stroke: \"#ff0000\",\n    width: 1,\n});\nfigure.point(x0, fx0, { radius: 3, fill: \"#ff0000\", stroke: \"#ff0000\" });\nfigure.point(x, fx, { radius: 3, fill: \"#ff0000\", stroke: \"#ff0000\" });\n\nfigure.plot((t) => tangentSlope * (t - x0) + fx0, {\n    domain: [-1, 5],\n    samples: 601,\n    stroke: \"#4169e1\",\n    width: 1,\n});\n\nfunction addLine(x1, y1, x2, y2, dash) {\n    const element = figure.line()\n        .x1(x1).y1(y1)\n        .x2(x2).y2(y2)\n        .stroke(\"#000000\");\n\n    if (dash) element.setAttribute(\"stroke-dasharray\", dash);\n}\n\nfunction addDoubleArrow(x1, y1, x2, y2) {\n    addLine(x1, y1, x2, y2);\n\n    if (y1 === y2) {\n        figure.polygon()\n            .points(`${x1},${y1} ${x1 + 6},${y1 - 3} ${x1 + 6},${y1 + 3}`)\n            .fill(\"#000000\");\n        figure.polygon()\n            .points(`${x2},${y2} ${x2 - 6},${y2 - 3} ${x2 - 6},${y2 + 3}`)\n            .fill(\"#000000\");\n    } else {\n        const direction = Math.sign(y2 - y1);\n        figure.polygon()\n            .points(`${x1},${y1} ${x1 - 3},${y1 + 6 * direction} ${x1 + 3},${y1 + 6 * direction}`)\n            .fill(\"#000000\");\n        figure.polygon()\n            .points(`${x2},${y2} ${x2 - 3},${y2 - 6 * direction} ${x2 + 3},${y2 - 6 * direction}`)\n            .fill(\"#000000\");\n    }\n}\n\naddLine(xmid + x0 * scale, ymid - fx0 * scale, xmid + x0 * scale, ymid, \"3\");\naddLine(xmid + x * scale, ymid - fx * scale, xmid + x * scale, ymid, \"3\");\naddLine(xmid + x0 * scale, ymid - fx0 * scale, xmid + 5 * scale, ymid - fx0 * scale, \"3\");\naddLine(xmid + x * scale, ymid - fx * scale, xmid + 5 * scale, ymid - fx * scale, \"3\");\naddLine(xmid + x * scale, ymid - tangentAtX * scale, xmid + 5 * scale, ymid - tangentAtX * scale, \"3\");\n\nfigure.label(String.raw`y=f\\left(x\\right)`, {\n    x: xmid + 2.5 * scale,\n    y: ymid - 3 * scale,\n    width: 5 * 14,\n    height: 22,\n    latex: true,\n});\n\naddDoubleArrow(\n    xmid + x0 * scale,\n    ymid - scale,\n    xmid + x * scale,\n    ymid - scale,\n);\nfigure.label(String.raw`\\Delta x=\\mathrm{d} x`, {\n    x: xmid + 1.85 * scale+6,\n    y: ymid - scale,\n    width: 5 * 14,\n    height: 22,\n    latex: true,\n});\n\naddDoubleArrow(\n    xmid + 3.6 * scale,\n    ymid - tangentAtX * scale,\n    xmid + 3.6 * scale,\n    ymid - fx0 * scale,\n);\nfigure.label(String.raw`\\mathrm{d} y`, {\n    x: xmid + 3.5 * scale+8,\n    y: ymid - 1.7 * scale,\n    width: 2 * 14,\n    height: 22,\n    latex: true,\n});\n\naddDoubleArrow(\n    xmid + 4.25 * scale,\n    ymid - fx * scale,\n    xmid + 4.25 * scale,\n    ymid - fx0 * scale,\n);\nfigure.label(String.raw`\\Delta y`, {\n    x: xmid + 4.25 * scale+5,\n    y: ymid - 1.7 * scale,\n    width: 2 * 14,\n    height: 22,\n    latex: true,\n});\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"xyxx+dxy=f(x)Δx=dxdyΔydy, the change in height to the tangent line, approximates Δy the change in height to the graph of f.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"240\" height=\"150\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 150\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"240,120 233,116 233,124\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"40,0 36,7 44,7\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"226\" y=\"90\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"48\" y=\"-2\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>y</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"116\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"124\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"113\" y=\"128\" width=\"14\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"116\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"124\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"160\" y=\"128\" width=\"140\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><polyline points=\"0,99 