9999994 235.2,17.400000000000006 235.6,17.200000000000003 236,17 236.4,16.799999999999997 236.8,16.599999999999994 237.2,16.400000000000006 237.60000000000002,16.19999999999999 238,16 238.4,15.799999999999997 238.79999999999998,15.600000000000009 239.20000000000002,15.399999999999991 239.60000000000002,15.199999999999989 240,15\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#4169e1\" stroke-width=\"1\"></polyline><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"36\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"36\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"36\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"45\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><foreignObject x=\"140\" y=\"0\" width=\"70\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"120,80 126,77 126,83\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"180,80 174,77 174,83\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"120\" y=\"80\" width=\"70\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"184\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"184\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"184,45 181,51 187,51\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"184,75 181,69 187,69\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"188\" y=\"52\" width=\"28\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"36\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"210,36 207,42 213,42\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"210,75 207,69 213,69\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"215\" y=\"52\" width=\"28\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></div></foreignObject></svg><figcaption><math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, the change in height to the tangent line, approximates <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> the change in height to the graph of <math><mi>f</mi></math>.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"### Some Questions\n\nIs a function ``f`` defined on an interval ``I`` necessarily the derivative of some other function defined on ``I``? The answer is no; some functions are derivatives and some are not. Although a derivative need not be a continuous function, it must, like a continuous function, have the intermediate-value property: on an interval ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, a derivative ``f^\\prime\\left(x\\right)`` takes on every value between ``f^\\prime\\left(a\\right)`` and ``f^\\prime\\left(b\\right)``. We will prove this in a next section.\n\nIf ``g\\left(x\\right)`` is continuous on an interval ``I``, then ``g\\left(x\\right)=f^\\prime\\left(x\\right)`` for some function ``f`` that is differentiable on ``I`` . We will discuss this fact further in Lecture 6.","output":"","result":"### Some Questions\n\nIs a function ``f`` defined on an interval ``I`` necessarily the derivative of some other function defined on ``I``? The answer is no; some functions are derivatives and some are not. Although a derivative need not be a continuous function, it must, like a continuous function, have the intermediate-value property: on an interval ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, a derivative ``f^\\prime\\left(x\\right)`` takes on every value between ``f^\\prime\\left(a\\right)`` and ``f^\\prime\\left(b\\right)``. We will prove this in a next section.\n\nIf ``g\\left(x\\right)`` is continuous on an interval ``I``, then ``g\\left(x\\right)=f^\\prime\\left(x\\right)`` for some function ``f`` that is differentiable on ``I`` . We will discuss this fact further in Lecture 6.","resultHTML":"<h3 id=\"md-y2ubvb-1531-17610rb-some-questions\">Some Questions</h3>\n<p>Is a function <math><mi>f</mi></math> defined on an interval <math><mi>I</mi></math> necessarily the derivative of some other function defined on <math><mi>I</mi></math>? The answer is no; some functions are derivatives and some are not. Although a derivative need not be a continuous function, it must, like a continuous function, have the intermediate-value property: on an interval <math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math>, a derivative <math><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo class=\"tml-prime tml-lrg-pad\">′</mo></msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> takes on every value between <math><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo class=\"tml-prime tml-lrg-pad\">′</mo></msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo class=\"tml-prime tml-lrg-pad\">′</mo></msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. We will prove this in a next section.</p>\n<p>If <math><mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> is continuous on an interval <math><mi>I</mi></math>, then <math><mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo class=\"tml-prime tml-lrg-pad\">′</mo></msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> for some function <math><mi>f</mi></math> that is differentiable on <math><mi>I</mi></math> . We will discuss this fact further in Lecture 6.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null}]